      "problem": "${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{cos}^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10{cos}^{2}⁡x-4{x}^{3}+10{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10\\cdot 1-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }(10-4{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(10x-{x}^{4}){|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =[10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}]-[10\\cdot 0-{0}^{4}] \\\\  & =10\\pi -{\\pi }^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
