    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-51122",
      "knowledge_point_id": "19a857ac-d36b-5753-a532-4f2a75612d85",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Using the Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "We use a variation of the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,{cos}^{2}⁡x=1-{sin}^{2}⁡x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we rewrite the denominator and calculate the resulting integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{1}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int ({sec}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+C"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+C"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C",
          "choices_complete": true,
