      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Using the Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }({cos}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =0-0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$",
