    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-352619",
      "knowledge_point_id": "19a857ac-d36b-5753-a532-4f2a75612d85",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Using the Pythagorean Trigonometric Identity",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "We use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x=1\\,⟹\\,1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we compute the given integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\operatorname{tan}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =2[\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sec}⁡(0)] \\\\  & =2(2-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "2"
            }
