    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-10568",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fe5822b4-c5d4-59a8-bd72-a41d3074e4e6",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Trigonometric Expressions With Linear Arguments Using the Pythagorean Identities",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\int (9{tan}^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )-5{x}^{4}+9)\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the identity  $1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =(5x+\\pi ),$ we get \n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )={sec}^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (9{tan}^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )-5{x}^{4}+9)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (9[1+{tan}^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )]-5{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (9{sec}^{2}⁡(5x+\\pi )-5{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi ))-5\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(5x+\\pi )-{x}^{5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
