    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-352714",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fe5822b4-c5d4-59a8-bd72-a41d3074e4e6",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Trigonometric Expressions With Linear Arguments Using the Pythagorean Identities",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "From the identity $1-{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta $ with $\\theta =x+\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get \n\n$$\n1-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})={sin}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{1-{cos}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{csc}^{2}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{4})-[-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(0+\\frac{\\pi }{4})] \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
