      "math_academy_id": "q-352747",
      "knowledge_point_id": "fe5822b4-c5d4-59a8-bd72-a41d3074e4e6",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating Trigonometric Expressions With Linear Arguments Using the Pythagorean Identities",
      "problem": "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}[4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+3{x}^{2}+4{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)]\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "From the identity ${sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1$ with $\\theta =\\frac{x}{2}-1,$ we get \n\n$$\n{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[4{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+3{x}^{2}+4{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)]\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{2}+4({sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1)+{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}-1))]\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}+4x]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{3}+4(1)]-[{0}^{3}+4(0)] \\\\  & =5-0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
